 ## Overview
-This method converts two-dimensional surface geometry into a three-dimensional voxel grid by performing three rasterization passes along the principal axis-aligned directions (X, Y, and Z) [S1, unofficial]. It produces a 6-separating voxelization, which is sparse enough to support 6-connected ray marching without penetrating the original surface [S1, unofficial]. It is distinguished from 26-separating methods by its sparsity, and from other 6-separating approaches like Schwarz and Seidel [SS10] by being minimal, as it corresponds exactly to the crosshairs intersection target rather than a superset [S1, unofficial].
+This method converts two-dimensional surface geometry into a three-dimensional voxel grid by performing three rasterization passes along the principal axis-aligned directions (X, Y, and Z) [S1, unofficial]. It produces a 6-separating voxelization, which is sparse enough to support 6-connected ray marching without penetrating the original surface [S1, unofficial]. It is distinguished from 26-separating methods by its sparsity, and from other 6-separating approaches like Schwarz and Seidel [SS10] by being minimal, as it corresponds exactly to the crosshairs intersection target rather than a superset [S1, unofficial]. It differs from [Triangle Projection Voxelization](5432-triangle-projection-voxelization.md) by using axis-aligned projections instead of arbitrary view directions, and from [Chunked UV Rasterization with Face ID Storage](2598-chunked-uv-rasterization-with-face-id-storage.md) by processing the entire grid in three global passes rather than localized chunks.
 
